<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n7799" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">chīlĭarchēs</span></b> ,
			ae, or <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">chīlĭarchus</span></b> , i,
		m., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">χιλιάρχης</span> or <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">χιλίαρχος</span>, <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a commander of</i> 1000 <i>soldiers</i>, <i>a chiliarch</i>, <i><span class="en">Curt. 5</span></i>.
			2, 2. Also in gen., <i>a head of</i> 1000 <i>persons</i>, Cod. Th. 16, 10, 20, §
			4.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> Among the
			Persians, <i>the highest officer of state next to the
				king</i>, <i>chancellor of state</i>, <i><span class="en">Nep. Con. 3,
			2</span></i>.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
